    \u0447\u0435\u043b\u043e\u0432\u0435\u0447\u043d\u043e\u0433\u043e \u0438 \u0431\u0435\u0441\u043a\u043e\u043d\u0435\u0447\u043d\u043e
    \u0443\u0432\u043b\u0435\u043a\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c\u043d\u043e\u0433\u043e \u0447\u0435\u043b\u043e\u0432\u0435\u043a\u0430."
  interestingFactsEn: "John von Neyman's life was full of not only great discoveries, but also amazing, amusing, and sometimes shocking episodes around him, and his phenomenal abilities, eccentricity and witty wit were created by many stories that walked Princeton and Los Alamos as legends, and these are the brightest of them in detail.\n\nThis is the most famous category of facts about von Neiman. His colleagues were convinced that he was thinking not as a human being, but as a machine that also has infinite memory. The story of the story of the two cities was written by heart: One day at a party in Princeton, someone argued that no one could quote by heart the first page of Charles Dickens' novel \"Shut Up About Two Cities\".And when the guests came to the surprise, he shook his shoulders and said, \"It's just a start, and you'd be surprised if I read it in its entirety.\" And he could really read by heart whole chapters of books that he read once as a child. Classic of Greek: At the age of six, he could quote in the ancient Greek language of Fukidida, an ancient Greek historian, and he confused university professors who came to visit his father.Phone book: The family was having fun asking little Janosh to find a phone number for anyone in Budapest, just calling the street. The boy, looking into the book for a second, immediately called the number, and then he could list a few dozen numbers in a row from memory.When the cyclists meet, how far will the fly fly go?\" A classic problem that is often solved by summating an infinite row.\n\n===Screams==========================================================================================]=====================================================================================================================================)))))))))===============The neighbors and colleagues complained, but the director of the institute did not decide to make a comment. Von Neyman himself said that loud music turned off the noises and helped him think fully. He could sit around the table for hours while his office heard the sound of Valkyrie.And he could, in the middle of talking about politics, suddenly shut up, walk up to the board and start to write it in formulas, explaining another scientific idea, and then, by destroying everything, go back to talking like nothing ever happened. The chauffeur and the Yankees: Being a very wealthy man, he always drove expensive cars.In Los Alamos, where the most secret was and everyone was walking around in the same military \"jeeps,\" he insisted that he be given a separate long limo. The guards were shocked at first but used to it. Colleagues joked that the appearance of an elegant limo made it clear that Big Boss had arrived.It was said that he could fall asleep on a half-word during the scientific report, and when he was woken up five minutes later, he immediately entered into a discussion and suggested a solution that had eluded the rapporteur for hours.\n\n♪ Strouma, anecdotes, and sense of humor Fon Neuman was known for his sharp, sometimes sarcastic, but always brilliant sense of humor. ♪ The anecdotes in the exams: He liked to tell jokes (including political and free-wing) during the lectures. ♪ On one occasion, during his defense, Von Neyman realized that he was on the verge of failure ♪He said, \"It's easy to prove it.If you have two socks -- one black, one white, and you lost them -- probability theory says you'll find a black one, and life shows you will find a white one you don't need.\" The answer to the criticism is, when one of the pure mathematicians criticized him for being an \"applied rather than true math,\" von Neyman replied with a smile: \"Maths aren't pure or applied. It's the right or wrong thing to do.And if it's the right one, it'll find its way sooner or later.\"\n\n♪ \"Extraordinary\" abilities and mistakes sometimes his phenomenal mind led to funny chickens, especially when he was wrong or confronted with something he couldn't understand. ♪ A dispute with a colleague once said that there was no limit to human memory. ♪ Fon Neiman was playing ♪ No, I do. I, for example, can't remember what I ate for breakfast two days ago, because it doesn't matter.\"However, on the same day, he re-sold the content of a three-volume monograph on the history he had read 20 years ago, word for word. The Goedel incident: he once discussed with Kurt Goedel a complex logical problem. Godel offered him a few solutions. Fon Neiman smiled and said, \"Curt, you know, I'm never wrong about the calculations. Let me check your formulas.\"He took the sheet, quickly calculated and admitted that one of the options was true. Godel dry replied, \"You're only wrong about one thing, you think you're never wrong.\" Fon Neiman laughed and admitted that it was the best joke he'd heard in a month. The purchase of the house was incredibly rich, but he couldn't count the money at home.When his wife asked him how much it cost, he said, \"I don't know, but I think it's a good thing because the house has a basement where I can keep my formulas.\"\n\n♪ chess, games and virtuitiveness Fon Neyman was a gambler, but his gambling was always supported by mathematics. The theory of playing at the card table: He loved poker and bridge. And unlike many mathematicians, he played not by \"pure probability,\" but by \"human factor.\" He said, \"It's not someone who knows the probability theory that wins, it's someone who knows the bluff theory, but I know the probability theory and the bluff theory.\"He often won, but he lost on purpose to avoid hurting his partners. The chess computer party: long before the real chess programs showed up, he predicted that \"machine will play chess better than man.\" When asked how it was possible, he said, \"It's very simple. The machine doesn't get tired, does not get distracted, does not make mistakes, and mistakes are the only thing that makes a man strong in chess.So once the machine learns not to make mistakes, it will be invincible.\"\n\nL.A.: Funny and scary, even when working on the atomic bomb was completely secret, its character was bright. Absent the military uniform: in Los Alamos, all scientists were given a uniform. Fon Neiman appeared in his own suit and tie, saying, \"The gentleman can't work in pajamas, even if the pajamas are of protective colour.\" And he was allowed to do it. Clara and the computer: His wife Clara worked with him on the EDVAC calculations.One day she made a mistake in the program, which caused the computer to produce an absurd result, and instead of being angry, Von Neuman looked at the page and said, \"Clara, honey, you made a mistake in line 42, but I love you more than this computer, so I forgive you, but if you're wrong again, I'll put you in the logarithms by hand.\"\n\nThe last chickens: the power of the spirit to the end, even in the face of his death, his mind and sense of humor did not leave him. The argument about biology: lying in a hospital with incurable cancer, he discussed cell biology with the doctor. The doctor said that cells divide and die, and the man is just a \"cell gun.\" Von Neyman smiled weakly and said, \"I always knew that I was a self-reproduction machine.It's only a pity that this particular copy is not going to be reproduced forever.\" Last words: rumored when he was already in agony, his brother asked him, \"Scared to die?\" Fon Neuman opened his eyes and said, \"Statistically, yes. But I've always wondered how it works. Now I know in practice.\" They say, after that he passed out and didn't wake up anymore.These stories create a portrait not just of a mathematical robot, but of a bright, emotional, human and infinitely fascinating person."
